## Solution Approach 2: Optimal (Monotonic Deque)

### Algorithm

1. Create an empty deque to store **indices** of elements.
2. Traverse the array using the `right` pointer.
3. Remove indices from the front of the deque if they are outside the current window.
4. Remove indices from the back whose corresponding values are smaller than the current element.
5. Add the current index to the back of the deque.
6. Once the first window is formed (`right >= k - 1`), the front of the deque contains the index of the maximum element.
7. Store the maximum in the answer array.
8. Return the answer array.

### Code

```java
class Solution {
    public int[] maxSlidingWindow(int[] nums, int k) {

        int n = nums.length;

        int[] ans = new int[n - k + 1];

        Deque<Integer> dq = new LinkedList<>();

        for (int right = 0; right < n; right++) {

            while (!dq.isEmpty() && dq.peekFirst() <= right - k) {
                dq.pollFirst();
            }

            while (!dq.isEmpty() && nums[dq.peekLast()] < nums[right]) {
                dq.pollLast();
            }

            dq.addLast(right);

            if (right >= k - 1) {
                ans[right - k + 1] = nums[dq.peekFirst()];
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
}
```

### Complexity

- **Time:** `O(n)`
- **Space:** `O(k)`
